          answer: 'The Drain of Wealth theory holds that constant flow of wealth from India to England without adequate return was the primary cause of Indian poverty. Dadabhai Naoroji described it as the "evil of all evils" and maintained Britain was "bleeding India white." The focal point was that national product was not available for Indian consumption or capital formation but drained away for political reasons. Home charges—salaries, pensions, interest on Sterling debt—were most significant. The drain prevented India from modernising its industrial structure, destroying handicrafts and transforming it into an importer of British manufactures. As Naoroji wrote, British capital came as loans, forcing India to pay interest on her own wealth.', page: 22,
