          answer: 'Commercialisation meant peasants produced agricultural crops for market sale rather than subsistence. British cash revenue demands, railway expansion, and industrial need for raw materials (cotton, indigo, opium, sugar) drove shifts from food crops to cash crops. This generated heavy rural indebtedness from moneylender borrowing, land alienation as peasants sold plots to pay debts, and growth of poverty. European enterprises monopolised production and distribution of cash crops, extracting profits that left little for cultivators.', page: 42,
