          answer: 'Labour conditions in colonial Indian factories were extremely harsh with 12-16 hour working days and very low wages. The Factories Act of 1881 was India\'s first major factory legislation, restricting child labour and limiting working hours. It was designed to protect children and provide health and safety measures for workers. The Indian Factories (Amendment) Act of 1891 provided weekly holidays for all workers, half-hour midday breaks, restricted women\'s working hours to 11 per day, and limited children\'s working hours to 7 per day. Wages began rising during 1873-1903. In the inter-war period there was some improvement in real earnings, recording a 45% rise in real wages, excluding unemployment losses during depression.', page: 102,
