          id: '104_4_8_1',
          question: 'Describe the development of railways in India and their economic and political significance.',
          answer: 'The initiative for railway construction came from Lord Dalhousie, who as Governor-General dispatched his famous minute in July 1850 emphasizing the great social, political and commercial benefits expected from railways. He urged creation of a system of trunk lines connecting the interior of each Presidency with its principal port and connecting different Presidencies with each other. The first railway line was opened from Bombay to Thana on 16th April 1853. Seven guaranteed companies became operational between 1853 and 1862: Great Indian Peninsular Railway, East Indian Railway, Madras Railway, Bombay-Baroda-Central India Railway, Sind-Punjab-Delhi Railway, Great Southern India Railway, and Eastern Bengal Railway. By 1905, the Indian Railways consisted of a total route of 39,834 km opened for traffic with a capital outlay of Rs 3,300 million. In 1905, the railway branch of the Public Works Department was abolished and a Railway Board was established. Railways began yielding net returns from 1900 onwards. The Indian Railways employed over a million people, the largest employer in the organized sector. By 1946-47, 78 percent of India\'s total area fell within the range of the railway system.', page: 119,
